# Line-ending and merge-attribute pinning for a cross-platform repo.
#
# Context: this project is developed on a mix of Windows (primary build
# host since the 2026-05-07 pivot in docs/findings.md), Linux EC2
# (fallback build host), and Graviton arm64 (runtime host). Without
# explicit eol pinning, Git on Windows defaults to autocrlf=true and
# every cross-platform commit produces noisy "LF→CRLF" diffs.
#
# Reference: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_end_of_line_conversion

# Default: treat as text, normalise to LF in the repo.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native shells must keep CRLF on checkout; cmd.exe and
# PowerShell are CRLF-strict for some constructs.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# POSIX shells must keep LF; CRLF breaks shebangs and `set -e` parsing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown / YAML / TOML / JSON: LF is the common-denominator convention.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# C / Make: LF — matches both QNX SDP 8.0 host toolchain and Linux.
*.c   text eol=lf
*.h   text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
*.mk  text eol=lf

# Binary types — never touch (most are also gitignored, but pin anyway
# in case something slips through).
*.bin  binary
*.img  binary
*.vmdk binary
*.iso  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary
